==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|quaken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cwacian|t=to quake, tremble, chatter}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kwakōną|t=to shake, quiver, tremble}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷog-|t=to shake, swing}}, related to {{cog|ang|cweċċan|t=to shake, swing, move, vibrate, shake off, give up}} (see {{l|en|quitch}}), <!--{{etyl|frs|-}} {{term|kwakkelje|t=to flounder, limp|lang=frs}},--> {{cog|nl|kwakkelen|t=to ail, be ailing}}, {{cog|de|Quackelei|t=chattering}}, {{cog|da|kvakle|t=to bungle}}, {{cog|la|vexō|t=toss, shake violently, jostle, vex}}, {{cog|ga|bogadh|t=a move, movement, shift, change}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kweɪk/|a=GenAm,RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-quake.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[tremble|trembling]] or [[shake|shaking]].
#: ''We felt a '''quake''' in the apartment every time the train went by''.
# An [[earthquake]], a trembling of the ground with force.
#: ''California is plagued by '''quakes'''; there are a few minor ones almost every month''.
#* {{quote-song|en|artist=Keith Whitley|year=1985|album=L.A. to Miami|title=Miami, My Amy|text=Well, everybody talks about the California '''quakes'''<br>But the first time I ever felt the earth shake<br>Was in Miami, when Amy touched me.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Something devastating, like a strong earthquake.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2024 January 24|author=Dyan Perry talks to Nick Brodrick|title=The industry has given me so much|journal=RAIL|issue=1001|page=44|text=But HS1 was more exposed to the COVID '''quake''' than most given its inherent reliance on international travel, which had collapsed, leaving cross-Channel operator Eurostar stacked with millions of debt.}}

