===Verb===
{{en-verb|past2=quoke|past2_qual=archaic|past3=quook|past3_qual=obsolete}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[tremble]] or [[shake]].
#: {{ux|en|I felt the ground '''quaking''' beneath my feet.}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|passage=Dorus threw Pamela behind a tree; where she stood '''quaking''' like the partridge on which the hawk is even ready to seize.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Nahum|1|5||The mountains '''quake''' at him, and the hills melt, and the earth is burnt at his presence.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To be in a state of fear, shock, amazement, etc., such as might cause one to tremble.
#* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|page=382|column=1|passage=Turning back, then, toward the basement staircase, she began to grope her way through blinding darkness, but had taken only a few uncertain steps when, of a sudden, she stopped short and for a little stood like a stricken thing, quite motionless save that she '''quaked''' to her very marrow in the grasp of a great and enervating fear.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|I|I|text=If Cupid have not spent all his quiver in / Venice, thou wilt '''quake''' for this shortly.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|III|II|text=Now could I drink hot blood / And do such bitter business as the bitter day / Would '''quake''' to look on.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|IV|VIII|text=Who honours not his father, Henry the fifth, that made all France to '''quake''', Shake he his weapon at us, and pass by.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ezekiel|12|18|text=Son of man, eat thy bread with '''quaking''', and drink thy water with trembling and carefulness.}}

